    "html": "<p>Retrieval-augmented generation is straightforward to demo and hard to productionize. The gap between the two is almost entirely about control: what the model may retrieve, what it may say, and which user is allowed to ask.</p>\n<h2>A modular retrieval pipeline</h2>\n<p>Treat retrieval as a pipeline of replaceable stages rather than one monolithic call:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ingestion</strong> — crawl websites and parse documents into normalized chunks.</li>\n<li><strong>Chunking</strong> — support fixed-size, recursive and semantic strategies, configurable per source.</li>\n<li><strong>Retrieval</strong> — hybrid search over vector and lexical indexes, with reranking.</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt construction</strong> — assemble grounded context with explicit source attribution.</li>\n<li><strong>Summarization</strong> — compress long contexts into answer-ready evidence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each stage should be independently testable and observable, because when answer quality drops you need to know whether ingestion, retrieval or generation is at fault.</p>\n<h2>Grounding before generation</h2>\n<p>An answer is only as trustworthy as its sources. Grounding means every claim in the response must map to retrieved evidence, and the system must abstain when evidence is missing. In practice this means structured prompt contracts, citation requirements, and post-generation checks that flag responses unsupported by retrieved context.</p>\n<h2>Guardrails, RBAC and masking</h2>\n<p>Three layers keep the system safe for real users:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>RBAC at retrieval time</strong> — filter documents by the requesting user&#39;s permissions <em>before</em> they ever reach the prompt, so the model cannot leak what the user should not see.</li>\n<li><strong>Sensitive-data masking</strong> — strip or tokenize PII in both retrieved context and model output.</li>\n<li><strong>LLM guardrails</strong> — schema-validate outputs, block off-topic or policy-violating responses, and route risky queries to refusal templates instead of free-form generation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These layers are not bolted on after launch; they shape the pipeline from day one. At HighLevel, an Agentic RAG system built with response grounding, RBAC, masking and guardrails was associated with measurably better product outcomes — 15% higher customer retention and 10% higher daily active users for the capability&#39;s surface.</p>\n<h2>Agents need protocols, not just prompts</h2>\n<p>Once retrieval works, the next step is letting agents act. Exposing existing APIs through an <strong>MCP (Model Context Protocol) server</strong> turns internal capabilities into tools agents can discover and call — with the same auth boundaries your application already enforces. Combined with event-driven processing and a centralized Redis store for rate limiting and access control, agentic features stay fast and contained under real traffic.</p>\n<h2>Takeaways</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Build retrieval as a modular pipeline; observe every stage.</li>\n<li>Ground every answer in evidence and allow abstention.</li>\n<li>Enforce RBAC at retrieval time and mask sensitive data in transit.</li>\n<li>Validate outputs with guardrails; expose capabilities to agents via MCP.</li>\n</ol>"
